## 3. Definition of done for a slice

<!-- The canonical list and its order are flagship-method references/planning-and-slices.md §6. Keep
this copy in the same order; add only this project's sizes, themes and content checks. -->

- [ ] **Plan:** screens and effects planned in DESIGN before building, with the test list and In /
      Not in; a risky slice had a skeptic review.
- [ ] **Contracts:** shared formats, ports and file ownership written into ARCHITECTURE before
      lanes split.
- [ ] **Build:** every new call read in the installed source. Visual work looked at ≥ 3 times
      across the look matrix with this project's sizes, themes, lightings and locales (§1);
      anything that moves watched on a simulator; the quality bar answered in writing. A content or
      data change read through `[TODO: review command]` the way a user meets it.
- [ ] **Integration:** one owner per rule; one latest version per saved document; stale wording
      swept; the integrated build cold-launched and looked at.
- [ ] **Analysis** clean; the full suite green, slow tests included, run now.
- [ ] **Review:** an adversarial review ran; reviewers re-ran and re-rendered.
- [ ] **Fixer:** each finding verified, then fixed with a guard or answered by a recorded decision.
- [ ] **Guards:** each new guard seen to fail with its fix removed, in an isolated copy, by
      assertion.
- [ ] **Final tree:** a confirming look at what the fixes touched; every gate re-run; generators and
      [TODO: the setup script, or "no setup script"] twice byte-identical; debug builds pass.
- [ ] **Docs:** DECISIONS, DESIGN "As built", ARCHITECTURE and §6 traps updated; PROGRESS updated
      (counts, hash, what was not verified, Device checks, the exact next step).
- [ ] **Commit:** one commit with a scoped message.

## 4. Handoff

End every session by rewriting PROGRESS in its eleven sections: Current phase / Done / In progress
(the exact next step) / Next / How to verify / Open questions / Device checks / END-OF-PROJECT LIST
/ Standing owner preferences / Traps hit / Small open items.
- Leave the tree green. Never commit red.
- **When low on capacity:** get green, write PROGRESS, record traps. Start nothing new. Write
  half-done work as "<slice> — in the working tree, not committed: <done / not done>".
- Record every side effect on a real external system (a probe that reached a production backend, a
  recording that wrote to a real account), and how the owner should treat it.

## 5. When stuck, and what to ask {{OWNER}}

1. Re-read the docs and the research reports. Read the real API source.
2. If it is a genuine decision, make the smallest safe assumption, record it in DECISIONS, and
   continue.
3. Before asking {{OWNER}} anything, check whether research, the genre or the platform already
   answers it.
   - Profile: [TODO: e.g. "a non-technical commissioner who wants you to decide design from
     evidence; ask only how a finished thing feels" OR "a developer who wants to co-decide
     architecture; offer options, each with a recommendation"] (DECISIONS #2).
   - Ask only what only {{OWNER}} can answer: money, accounts, legal identity, keys, how a finished
     thing feels, scope, release.
   - Ask in {{OWNER_LANGUAGE}}, briefly, as a numbered list, each item with your recommendation
     and its reason. Flag anything permanent.
   - A question waits for its moment in PROGRESS's "Open questions" table. Never wait on an answer:
     proceed on your recommendation, recorded as an ASSUMED DECISIONS entry, and build nothing
     permanent on it (§2, invariant 11).
4. Standing preferences and boundaries live in PROGRESS "Standing owner preferences". A memory note
   goes only into this project's own memory, never another project's.

